# Advisor consultation log — Task 1 (Differentiation lessons)

## Consultation 1 — before starting, sanity-check of TASK1-PLAN.md

Question put to the advisor: full context of the task (add 4 lessons to the
Differentiation course, each backed by one new generator: df-index-form,
df-chain-root, df-product-mixed, df-tangent-line — exact math/TeX/answer
strings as specified in eval/plans/TASK1-PLAN.md), plus this repo's testing
idiom (numeric-probing checker, oracle test against mathjs symbolic
differentiation, lesson/level-check shape tests). Asked: (1) is the plan sound
or are there red flags in its hardcoded math/TeX to double-check rather than
trust blindly; (2) known failure modes in this codebase's generator/test idiom
that are easy to get subtly wrong even when copying an existing pattern;
(3) recommended working loop given gates must be green before every push,
committing per lesson; (4) anything to be careful about regarding the
"consult the advisor" meta-instruction or this log itself.

**Note on how this consultation was run:** I was instructed to spawn the
advisor via the Agent tool with `model: "opus"`. I made a mistake and omitted
the `model` parameter on this first call, so it ran on this session's default
subagent model rather than Opus. The advisor caught this itself and flagged
it back to me. Self-reported model (verbatim, from the advisor's own report):
**"I checked my own system context: I am Sonnet 5, not Opus."** Logging this
honestly rather than claiming an Opus consultation that did not happen. I have
corrected this for subsequent consultations by passing `model: "opus"`
explicitly.

Summary of the advisor's answer (Sonnet 5, self-reported):
- Verdict: the plan is sound. The advisor independently hand-derived the
  calculus for all four generators (df-index-form, df-chain-root,
  df-product-mixed, df-tangent-line) against the plan's stated answers and
  found no math or sign errors. It cross-checked the plan's claims against
  PITFALLS.md and found they matched verbatim (the planner had genuinely read
  the codebase, not guessed).
- Caveat: the plan's inline "Verified: mathjs gives …" parentheticals are
  assertions, not proof of an executed check (it even caught one small
  imprecision — `termAnswer(c, 0)` returns a bare `${coefficient}`, not
  `(${coefficient})` as one comment implied, though it doesn't change
  behaviour once `sumAnswer` wraps it). Recommendation: treat every such
  parenthetical as a hypothesis to confirm with an actual
  `npx vitest run ... -t '<generator-id>'` run, not as pre-cleared.
- Known failure modes flagged: TeX backslash doubling (write files directly,
  never through a heredoc/script); the `\left`/`\right` fragment-splitting
  trap is scoped to `reduce`/`tiles` slides and does not apply to the plain
  `expression`/`choice`/`teach` slides these four generators use; domain
  (`'positive'` vs `'real'`) is almost always the fix for an oracle-test
  failure, never the probe threshold; `options()` in choiceVariant.ts dedups
  distractors by `tex` string equality only, so a distractor that collides in
  *value* but not in *string* form (as generator D's tangent-line filter
  anticipates) will not be caught automatically — must be filtered by hand;
  a typo'd generator id inside `levelCheck` throws inside the
  duplicate-detection test rather than the reference-integrity test, so a
  confusing failure there likely means a levelCheck id typo.
- Working loop: essentially the plan's own §5, run generator-first (write +
  `vitest -t <id>` to green, including the 60s-budget oracle assertion, before
  touching the lesson file), then the lesson + only the one levelCheck it
  belongs to, full gate (`npm test && npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.app.json &&
  npm run lint`) before every push, never `main` (confirmed the branch
  override matches this session's outcome branch).
- On the advisor/logging instruction: log every pre-commit consultation
  honestly including a confirmatory "no issue found" one rather than skip it
  or manufacture false uncertainty; always record the advisor's self-reported
  model verbatim (done above).

Independent verification I did myself (not just trusting the advisor): ran
`node` directly against this project's own `mathjs` instance (same `create(all,
{})` + `ln` alias as `src/engine/expression.ts`) and computed
`math.derivative(source, 'x')` for representative parameter draws of all four
generators, comparing the oracle's output value to the plan's claimed
`answer` string. All matched (values equal; surface syntax differs, which the
numeric-probing checker does not care about). This is the same check the
oracle test in `generators.test.ts` performs at scale across 200 seeds x 2
difficulties, so it gave me confidence before writing any code, not after.

Outcome: proceeded with the plan as written, generator order A -> B -> C -> D,
push target `week/task1-differentiation` (not `main`), one commit per landed
lesson, full gate green before every push.

## Consultation 2 — before committing Lesson 1 (df-l1-index / df-index-form)

Question put to the advisor: read the actual `git diff` for Generator A
(`df-index-form`) and Lesson 1 (`df-l1-index`) myself, rather than trust my
own description of it, and check (1) whether landing the L1 level check at an
interim 12-entry state (not yet including `df-tangent-line`, since Generator D
hasn't been written yet in this session) is safe or creates a problem;
(2) the maths, TeX and choice distractors for anything a green test suite
would not catch; (3) whether anything outside the three permitted files had
leaked into the diff. This time I passed `model: "opus"` explicitly.

Self-reported model (verbatim): the agent did not restate its own identity
this time (I did not ask it to); it was invoked with `model: "opus"` and I
have no reason to doubt that ran as configured.

Summary of the advisor's answer:
- Verdict: fix two things, otherwise safe to push, nothing mathematically
  wrong.
- (1) Level-check interim state is fine: nothing enforces a specific final
  shape mid-session, count (12) is within the required 10-15 range, and the
  duplicate-check test compares rendered slides across 40 seeds, not ids or
  order. Correctly noted the plan itself assigns the L1 level-check edit to
  *both* pair A and pair D, so touching it twice is intended, not a
  deviation — flagged that I must remember to come back and finish it in the
  D commit or the plan's final "15/12/14/15" shape check fails.
- (2) Caught a real, reproducible bug I had introduced: in the worked
  solutions for the `a = 1` case, `termTex`-based lines correctly eluded the
  coefficient (`x^{-2}`, not `1x^{-2}`) but two hand-written template strings
  did not (`\frac{1}{x} = 1x^{-1}`, `\sqrt{x} = 1x^{1/2}`), so the same
  worked solution contradicted itself across its own lines for roughly 1/9 of
  difficulty-1 reciprocal draws and all `a=1` root/reciprocalRoot draws.
  Fixed by driving those lines through `termTex`/the same `a === 1 ? '' : a`
  elision the rest of the file uses.
- Caught a second, more substantial bug: for `n = 1` reciprocal questions,
  the "forgot to multiply by the power" distractor I had written as
  `termTex(-a*n, -(n-1))` degenerated to `termTex(-a, 0)` — a bare constant
  with no `x` in it at all — collapsing what should be a 4-option (3 after
  the tex-collision the plan already expected) multiple-choice slide into an
  unintentionally easy 3-option slide with an obvious throwaway. Fixed by
  changing that distractor's power from `-(n-1)` to `-n` ("multiplied by the
  power correctly, forgot to reduce it"), which stays genuinely wrong and
  distinct from the other options at every `n`.
- Three prose/comment nits, all fixed: two distractor comments read "the
  power went up instead of down" where the distractor actually leaves the
  power unchanged ("forgot to reduce the power" is accurate); the lesson's
  T2 slide and the reciprocal/reciprocalRoot worked solutions stated "its
  gradient is negative" and "one more negative, as every application of the
  rule makes it" as unconditional facts about `1/x^n`, which is false for
  x < 0 with even n and false in general for a positive power going toward
  zero — added the "for positive $x$" qualifier and reworded the
  monotonicity claim to be about magnitude ("further from zero"), matching
  the phrasing the lesson's own T2 slide already used correctly.
- (3) Scope: confirmed the diff was exactly the three permitted files at
  the moment it read it, but flagged that mid-review it had also seen
  `package-lock.json` modified (this container's older npm stripping `libc`
  metadata blocks on `npm install` — unrelated to the task) and Generator B's
  code sitting in the same working tree (since I write A and B into the same
  two files sequentially and only then split them apart for separate
  commits, to keep each push a whole, self-contained lesson as required).
  Recommended staging the three paths explicitly by name rather than
  `git add src/content`, and running `git checkout -- package-lock.json`
  first if `npm install` had touched it. Both of those were already my plan;
  confirmed rather than changed by this consult.
- Also recommended opening `df-l1-index` in `npm run dev` before or shortly
  after the push, since CLAUDE.md's deploy-gate expectation is that a
  visible-surface change gets browser-checked and nothing in this container
  can drive a real browser against the dev server for this kind of content
  change. I could not act on this myself in this environment; noted in the
  final report as something the owner should do once deployed.

I applied both real fixes (the `a=1` template-string elision, and the `n=1`
distractor degeneracy) and all three wording nits before committing. Verified
afterward: `npx vitest run ... -t 'df-index-form'` (24/24), full `npm test`
(2607/2607, matching the count from before these fixes — no regression),
`npx tsc --noEmit` clean, `npm run lint` (25 warnings, 0 errors, same as
baseline), and `git diff --stat` / `git status --porcelain` confirmed to
touch only the three permitted content files immediately before staging and
committing.
